Silver Clinic Clinic has been my favourite part of my training: the opportunity to work with patients who are struggling with everyday life, to see how a simple movement can make a big difference in their lives. That was the focus of my undergraduate work, where I completed a Project Management Doctorate project within the context of rehabilitation. One of the highlights was a recent case where a family member had been struggling to get moving, and I was able to help them develop a new mobility tool that made everyday movement feel more like a breeze. This was followed by a series of projects that strengthened my understanding of how assessment, rehabilitation, and recovery can be linked to a personalised approach to treatment. In particular, I completed a new diagnostic tool for treating a common issue in my own practice, and I have also completed several projects that strengthened my ability to apply what I had learned in a clinical setting to a real-life situation. Through this, I have deepened my understanding of the importance of collaboration, the iterative nature of assessment, and the value of clear communication in the clinical setting. Alongside my work as a PhD student, I have also completed 4 projects that have helped me develop my thinking and approach to research, and I am looking forward to contributing to a wide range of projects that will help me contribute to a more comprehensive and sustainable approach to clinical assessment and treatment.

In addition, I have completed a Doctorate at a university, and I am looking forward to contributing to a wide range of projects that will help me develop my thinking and approach to research, and I will be able to contribute to a more comprehensive and sustainable approach to clinical assessment and treatment. In particular, I have developed the ability to:

* Apply new thinking and evidence-based approaches to assessment;
* Apply clinical reasoning and evidence-based reasoning to clinical decision-making;
