Tuesday in my eyes, the difference between a busy warehouse and a fast-moving shift is the difference between a busy day and a busy week. I am drawn to people, processes, and the challenge of turning day-to-day tasks into systems that make the difference in both business and operations. This is what I want to do with my career: to bring together the practical aspects of warehouse operations with the speed, accuracy, and focus needed to get the job done efficiently, safely, and on schedule.

My academic journey began with a Master’s degree from a university ranked in the global top 500, and I completed 8 projects with tangible outcomes, each reinforcing the importance of structured processes and continuous improvement. In one role, I worked with a commonly used warehouse operational tool, which I now use to troubleshoot delays and make decisions quickly. Colleagues have also taken notice of my approach, commenting on how they now understand how to use the system effectively, and I have been encouraged to consider what I can do to add value in the role.

Across my work, I have also completed 8 recurring operational tools, including:
  - A new warehouse operating system that I now use to troubleshoot delays and make decisions quickly;
  - A way to manage inventory and workflow flows, so I can keep track of what is happening and when it is ready to move on to the next step;
  - A way to improve workflow and improve throughput, so I can keep track of what is happening and when it is ready to move on to the next step;
  - A way to manage shift work, so I can keep track of when and where it is needed, and when it is ready to move on to the next step.

